    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p30-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graphs of $y=\\log_2x$ and $y=(\\log_2x)^2$ on the same set of axes. Write down the maximal domain and the range of $f(x)=(\\log_2x)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\operatorname{dom}f=(0,\\infty)$; $\\operatorname{ran}f=[0,\\infty)$. The graphs intersect at $(1,0)$ and $(2,1)$.",
          "worked": "The logarithm requires $x>0$. Its square is non-negative and equals $0$ at $x=1$, so $\\operatorname{dom}f=(0,\\infty)$ and $\\operatorname{ran}f=[0,\\infty)$.\nFor the intersections, set $t=\\log_2x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2&=t\\\\t(t-1)&=0\\\\t&=0\\text{ or }1\\\\x&=1\\text{ or }2\\end{align*}$$\nThe intersection points are $(1,0)$ and $(2,1)$. The logarithm increases throughout its domain. Its square decreases from $+\\infty$ to $(1,0)$, then increases to $+\\infty$. Both have vertical asymptote $x=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p30-q6-a-sol",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"seriesBlue\",\"hex\":\"268CFF\",\"reason\":\"squared logarithmic function\"},{\"name\":\"seriesRed\",\"hex\":\"EF6068\",\"reason\":\"logarithmic function\"}],\"reference\":\"page 30 question 6(a)\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.8cm]\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\\definecolor{seriesBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}\\definecolor{seriesRed}{HTML}{EF6068}\\path[use as bounding box] (-0.8,-3.5) rectangle (6.4,5);\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-0.5,0)--(4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-3)--(0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};\\node[below left] at (0,-3) {$x=0$};\\begin{scope}\\clip (0,-3) rectangle (4,4.2);\\draw[seriesRed,line width=0.8pt,domain=0.125:4,samples=140] plot (\\x,{ln(\\x)/ln(2)});\\draw[seriesBlue,line width=0.8pt,domain=0.24:4,samples=140] plot (\\x,{(ln(\\x)/ln(2))^2});\\end{scope}\\node[seriesRed,right] at (4,2) {$y=\\log_2x$};\\node[seriesBlue,right] at (4,4) {$y=(\\log_2x)^2$};\\fill (1,0) circle (1.4pt) node[below right] {$(1,0)$};\\fill (2,1) circle (1.4pt) node[above left] {$(2,1)$};\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 75,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 30,
                "sourceRegion": "6(a)",
                "description": "Shared axes compare the logarithm and its square, with both intersection points and matched curve labels.",
                "mathematics": "y=log_2x and y=(log_2x)^2 on x>0; intersections (1,0),(2,1); square has minimum zero.",
                "answerVisibility": "solution-only"
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 75
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $x$ for which $(\\log_2x)^2>\\log_2x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<x<1$ or $x>2$.",
          "worked": "Let $t=\\log_2x$, with $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2&>t\\\\t(t-1)&>0\\\\t&<0\\text{ or }t>1\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\log_2x$ is increasing, $\\log_2x<0$ gives $0<x<1$, and $\\log_2x>1$ gives $x>2$. Thus $0<x<1$ or $x>2$, corresponding to where the squared-logarithm curve is above the logarithm curve.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
